          "type": "assistant",
          "text": "Now I have everything I need. Here's the complete workflow.\n\n# How this system works (mental model)\n\nThis project is your **single-user, local Kanban + terminal dashboard** for driving Claude Code (or yourself) through development work. The building blocks:\n\n- **Initiative** (optional, top-level) → **Epic** (a big chunk of work) → **Work item** (task / bug / sub-task).\n- Work items move through states: `triage` → `ready` → `in_progress` → `in_review` → `done` (or `canceled`).\n- You don't insert DB rows directly. You **drop JSON envelopes** into `data/ingest/inbox/`, and a file watcher inside the dev server picks them up and writes to SQLite.\n- The `npm run ingest` CLI is a convenience wrapper that builds & writes those JSON envelopes for you.\n- The browser UI at `http://localhost:5173` lets you see the Kanban board, open task detail, drag between columns, etc.\n\nSo the loop is: **ingest (create things) → view in UI → transition status → work on code → mark done.**\n\n---\n\n# Step 0 — One-time setup\n\nRun these each as a separate Bash invocation from the project root:\n\n```bash\nnpm install\n```\n```bash\nnpm run dev\n```\n\nLeave `npm run dev` running. The server listens on `:3001` (with the ingest watcher), the Vite client on `:5173`. Open **http://localhost:5173** in your browser.\n\nIf you've been experimenting, you can wipe first:\n```bash\nnpx tsx scripts/wipe.ts --scope all --yes\n```\n\n---\n\n# Step 1 — Create the Poker epic\n\nIn a second terminal (keep the dev server running):\n\n```bash\nnpm run ingest -- epic --title \"Online Poker Game\" --description \"Browser-based multiplayer Texas Hold'em playable with friends over the internet.\"\n```\n\nThe CLI prints JSON like:\n```json\n{ \"file\": \".../inbox/epic-online-poker-game-xxxxxxxx.json\", \"entity_id\": \"<EPIC_UUID>\", \"event_type\": \"epic.created\" }\n```\n\n**Copy that `entity_id`** — you'll pass it as `--epic-id` to every task below. Let's call it `$EPIC_ID`.\n\nYou can set a shell var for convenience:\n```bash\nEPIC_ID=<paste-the-uuid-here>\n```\n\nRefresh the UI — the epic should appear.\n\n---\n\n# Step 2 — Create tasks under the epic\n\nEach of the following is one `npm run ingest` call. Run them one at a time so you can capture the returned `entity_id` if you want to add comments or sub-tasks later. I've split the game into 12 incremental, shippable tasks.\n\n### Phase A — Project skeleton\n\n```bash\nnpm run ingest -- task --title \"Scaffold poker repo (client + server workspaces)\" --kind task --epic-id $EPIC_ID --tags \"setup,backend,frontend\" --acceptance-criteria \"New repo poker-game/ with /server (Node+TS+Express+ws) and /client (Vite+React+TS). npm run dev starts both.\"\n```\n```bash\nnpm run ingest -- task --title \"Pick and install core deps\" --kind task --epic-id $EPIC_ID --tags \"setup\" --acceptance-criteria \"Server: express, ws, zod, uuid, tsx. Client: react, react-router, zustand. Shared: pokersolver for hand evaluation.\"\n```\n\n### Phase B — Game engine (pure logic, no network)\n\n```bash\nnpm run ingest -- task --title \"Implement 52-card deck + shuffle (Fisher-Yates, seedable)\" --kind task --epic-id $EPIC_ID --tags \"engine\" --acceptance-criteria \"deck.ts exports createDeck(), shuffle(deck, seed?). Unit tests verify 52 unique cards and deterministic shuffle with seed.\"\n```\n```bash\nnpm run ingest -- task --title \"Texas Hold'em hand evaluator integration\" --kind task --epic-id $EPIC_ID --tags \"engine\" --acceptance-criteria \"Wrap pokersolver to rank 5-of-7 hands. Tests: straight flush beats four of a kind, kicker tiebreakers.\"\n```\n```bash\nnpm run ingest -- task --title \"Betting round state machine\" --kind task --epic-id $EPIC_ID --tags \"engine\" --acceptance-criteria \"State: pre-flop → flop → turn → river → showdown. Actions: fold/check/call/bet/raise. Side-pot math for all-ins. Unit-tested with scripted hands.\"\n```\n\n### Phase C — Server (WebSocket + rooms)\n\n```bash\nnpm run ingest -- task --title \"WebSocket server with room/table management\" --kind task --epic-id $EPIC_ID --tags \"backend\" --acceptance-criteria \"POST /tables creates a table, returns join URL. ws://.../tables/:id joins. Server tracks seats, dealer button, pot, current player.\"\n```\n```bash\nnpm run ingest -- task --title \"Game event protocol (player→server, server→player)\" --kind task --epic-id $EPIC_ID --tags \"backend,protocol\" --acceptance-criteria \"Typed messages: JOIN, SIT, ACTION(fold|check|call|bet|raise), STATE_UPDATE, HAND_RESULT. Zod-validated. Private hole cards sent only to owning player.\"\n```\n```bash\nnpm run ingest -- task --title \"Reconnect + turn timeout handling\" --kind task --epic-id $EPIC_ID --tags \"backend\" --acceptance-criteria \"Player can disconnect and rejoin within 60s without losing seat. 30s action timer auto-folds on timeout.\"\n```\n\n### Phase D — Client UI\n\n```bash\nnpm run ingest -- task --title \"Lobby screen: create table / join table by code\" --kind task --epic-id $EPIC_ID --tags \"frontend\" --acceptance-criteria \"Lobby has two buttons. Creating a table gives a shareable join code/URL. Entering a code joins that table.\"\n```\n```bash\nnpm run ingest -- task --title \"Poker table UI (seats, community cards, pot, chips)\" --kind task --epic-id $EPIC_ID --tags \"frontend\" --acceptance-criteria \"Up to 6 seats around an oval table. Shows each player's chip stack, their bet in front, 5 community card slots, pot total, dealer button.\"\n```\n```bash\nnpm run ingest -- task --title \"Action controls (fold/check/call/bet/raise with slider)\" --kind task --epic-id $EPIC_ID --tags \"frontend\" --acceptance-criteria \"Buttons only enabled when it's your turn. Raise uses slider bound to [min-raise, stack]. Disabled actions greyed out with reason tooltip.\"\n```\n\n### Phase E — Ship it\n\n```bash\nnpm run ingest -- task --title \"Deploy server to Fly.io / Railway + host client\" --kind task --epic-id $EPIC_ID --tags \"deploy\" --acceptance-criteria \"Public URL reachable from outside your LAN. WSS (not WS) so modern browsers allow it. Friends can join a table from their own laptops.\"\n```\n\n---\n\n# Step 3 — The daily workflow\n\nFor each task, the rhythm is:\n\n1. **Open http://localhost:5173.** You'll see the Kanban board with all tasks in the `triage` column under the \"Online Poker Game\" epic.\n2. **Triage → Ready.** Drag a task to `ready` (or use the task detail dropdown) once you're sure it's well-scoped. This is where you'd refine acceptance criteria.\n3. **Ready → In Progress.** When you start coding, drag it to `in_progress`. If you want to add the branch name:\n   ```bash\n   # (the UI also lets you edit this in task detail)\n   ```\n4. **Work.** Build the thing in your *poker-game* repo (separate from this tool). Commit code there as normal.\n5. **Leave breadcrumbs.** Add comments as you go — future you will thank you:\n   ```bash\n   npm run ingest -- comment --work-item-id <TASK_UUID> --body \"Chose pokersolver over phe — simpler API, slightly slower but fine for 6-max.\"\n   ```\n6. **In Progress → In Review.** On transition to `in_review` the system can fire an adversarial-review prompt (v0.3.3 feature) — useful for catching gaps before you mark done.\n7. **In Review → Done.** Ship it, drag to `done`.\n\nRepeat until Phase E is green and you're dealing cards to your friends.\n\n---\n\n# Step 4 — Useful escape hatches\n\n- **Check watcher health:** `curl http://localhost:3001/api/ingest/status`\n- **See what was ingested:** files in `data/ingest/processed/` — rejected files land in `data/ingest/rejected/` with an error.\n- **Dry-run a wipe:** `npx tsx scripts/wipe.ts --scope tasks --dry-run`\n- **Seed sample data** to see the UI populated before committing your poker data: `npm run seed`\n\n---\n\n# Important clarification\n\n**This tool tracks the work; it does not host the game.** You'll build the actual poker game as a **separate codebase** (e.g. `~/GitHub/poker-game/`) with its own WebSocket server. The agentic task system just manages your backlog/Kanban/activity log while you build.\n\nIf you'd like, I can scaffold that `poker-game/` repo next so you have somewhere to actually write the code the tasks describe.",
